      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:18793/leetcode/329/#step2",
      "targetText": "03Make the waiting count visibleOur question: how can we respect dependencies without deep recursion? out counts larger neighbors not yet removed; the ready queue holds unremoved cells with out=0. Process only the queue present at the start of a round. Cells becoming ready during it wait for the next round. This is reverse topological layering: process cells with no remaining successors first.This is a browser teaching simulation, not execution of Python. Changing a preset restarts this experiment. Keep the matrix fixed and remove one round at a time: watch out decrease and finalized L accumulate. Back restores a whole-round snapshot. The ledger retains every completed round and every count change; no rounds are skipped.Matrix presetFour-cell snakeOfficial example 1Official example 2Greedy counterexampleAll equalSingle zeroEach cell starts with its original value. Remaining cells show out; “ready” marks the next round. Removed cells show L and ✓. Row/column coordinates start at 0. An orange border and the word “ready” both mark readiness; color does not encode value magnitude.(0,0)1out=2 · pending(0,1)2out=1 · pending(1,0)4out=0 · ready(1,1)3out=1 · pending← Previous roundRemove next round →Reset this experimentRemove to endCompleted 0/4 rounds; 0/4 cells finalized. Next-round coordinates: (1,0).No cells removed yet. First inspect local peaks with out=0."
