      "related": " #32: retain boundaries with an index stack",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:18793/leetcode/32/#step1",
      "targetText": "02Why can we skip work?How can we avoid pairing again from every start? In \")()())\", the close at index 0 cannot match; any later valid fragment must start to its right. Save these uncrossable boundary indices and the indices of still-unmatched openings. Use a Python list as a stack: add or remove only at its end. “Push” means append; “pop” removes and returns the last item; “top” means that last item. Start with −1 at the bottom, a virtual boundary before the string, and store unmatched opening indices above it. On (, append(i). On ), pop first; if the list becomes empty, use the current i as the new boundary.A suffix is a contiguous fragment ending at the current index i. If top t remains after the pop, every parenthesis after t through i is paired, giving length i−t. t is either an unmatched opening or the last invalid close; including it would break validity, so we cannot cross it. In the default example, just before i=4 the stack is [0,3]. This close matches index 3; popping leaves [0], so indices 1…4 have length 4−0=4. One calculation joins the two pairs without rescanning indices 1…2.best is the maximum over all processed endpoints; length is only the current valid suffix. The bottom boundary differs from the unmatched openings above it; −1 is not an input character.Sliding windows also preserve contiguity"
